A new year — and the country feels different from the one that entered 1962.

Goulart won his plebiscite. Eighty percent of Brazilians voted to restore full presidential powers, to bury the parliamentary compromise that had been imposed on him when he took office. It was a triumph, and he celebrated it as one. But a plebiscite is not a program. Goulart has his powers back. Now the question is what he will do with them.

The economy is the first answer, and it is not a reassuring one. Inflation, already severe, is accelerating past all the projections the technocrats in the Finance Ministry had offered. The *Plano Trienal* — the Three-Year Plan drafted by Celso Furtado — was meant to stabilize things while pressing forward with reform. Instead it has satisfied no one. The left finds it too timid. The right finds it too radical. The IMF finds it insufficiently austere. The workers find their wages eroding faster than any plan can address.

In the Northeast, the leagues are restless. In the barracks, the officers are meeting. In the American embassy, cables go back to Washington with assessments that grow grimmer with each dispatch.

We have perhaps a year. Perhaps less.

